  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1211 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nSeveral global models indicate the mid level ridge continuing to \nstrengthen during the long term thus reducing our chances for rain \nand storms. It eventually pushes off to the east across the TX/LA \nborder by the end of the week before meandering back west \nsouthwestward over our area over the weekend. The result is most \nareas should remain dry with just very spotty isolated showers and \nthunderstorms across the Coastal Plains and again across the Rio \nGrande as they remain on the periphery of the ridge. The good news \nis that with the ridge being close by it should drop our humidity \nvalues beginning Thursday as onshore flow from the Gulf will be \nreduced. While it will remain unseasonably hot across the area, heat \nindices will only likely be 1 to 3 degrees above the actual air \ntemperatures giving us some reprieve from the oppressive heat and \nhumidity we have experienced for most of this month. Unfortunately \nrain chances continue to look abysmal for most of the area as long \nas the ridge remains parked nearby.",
